### Choosing which service you target

`target-exec` and `target-logs` run against one member of the group at a time.
Switch between them yourself — no respawn, no second container:

```bash
# The group, the current target, and every running member
target-switch

# Point target-exec/target-logs at another member
target-switch <service>
```

The switch takes effect for every command you start afterwards. Members are
re-read from the running stack each time, so a service that restarted is still
reachable and one that is down is simply not listed.

The selection records a **container id**, so if your current target is recreated
(`compose up -d`, `--force-recreate`) `target-exec` starts failing with "no such
container". Re-run `target-switch <service>` to pick up the new container — the
listing is always current even when your selection has gone stale.
{% endif %}

### How the shared volume works

Both your `/workspace` and the service container mount from the **same host
directory**. However, the service container may mount only specific
subdirectories (not the entire repo). To find out what's shared:

{% if LMER_SERVICE_CONTAINER %}
{% if LMER_SERVICE_GROUP is defined and LMER_SERVICE_GROUP %}
(The container id below is the one this session launched with; after a
`target-switch` use the id that `target-switch` prints for the current target.)
{% endif %}
```bash
# See how the service container mounts the source
docker inspect {{ LMER_SERVICE_CONTAINER }} --format '{% raw %}{{json .Mounts}}{% endraw %}' | python3 -m json.tool

# Check the service container's working directory
target-exec pwd
```
{% else %}
```bash
# Once you have picked a target with target-switch, it prints that container's
# id — pass it to `docker inspect <id> --format '{% raw %}{{json .Mounts}}{% endraw %}'` to see how the
# service mounts the source, or ask the container directly:
target-exec pwd
```
{% endif %}

Edits to files under shared mount points are immediately visible in both
directions. Files outside the service container's mounts are only in `/workspace`.

### Running commands in the project environment

Use `target-exec` to run commands inside the service container (which has the
project's runtime, database connections, installed packages, etc.):

```bash
# Run tests
target-exec pytest tests/ -x

# Django management commands
target-exec python manage.py migrate
target-exec python manage.py shell

# Check installed packages
target-exec pip list

# Run arbitrary commands
target-exec <command> [args...]
```

### Viewing service logs

```bash
# Target service logs (follow mode)
target-logs

# Another container's logs
target-logs <container-name>

# Last N lines of a container
target-logs <container-name> 100
```

### Important notes

- **Edit files locally** in `/workspace` using normal tools (vim, sed, etc.)
- **Run project commands** via `target-exec` (pytest, manage.py, pip, etc.)
- **Git operations** run directly in `/workspace` (not via target-exec)
- The service container may only mount **specific subdirectories** — check mounts before assuming file visibility
